192-### 2026-10-07 — How to take step 2's 54 operator items (operator ruling)
193-- **Context:** threat model v0.3 (after review rounds 1 and 2 and two revisions; GLM r2 "fit", Codex r2 "not fit yet",
194-  every r2 finding then disposed in disposition-r2.md; neither reviewer has seen v0.3) carries OQ-1..17, RR-1..20,
195-  CR-1..17.
196-- **Chose:** B — the 17 open questions one at a time; each brief names the change requests and residual risks its
197-  ruling settles; the unlinked remainder afterwards, one at a time.
198-- **Why:** keeps one decision per message while cutting ~54 rulings to ~17 plus a tail. Scored B +42, C +31, A +1, D −8.
199-- **Rejected:** A (54 rounds, many restating earlier answers), C (batches risk acceptance, against the standing rule),
200-  D (risk acceptance is reserved to the operator by the role card).
201-
202-### 2026-10-07 — OQ-1 which added adversaries to confirm (operator ruling)
203-- **Chose:** A — confirm ADV-11 (compromised or rogue hub), ADV-12 (unadmitted joiner), ADV-13 (network attacker:
204-  delay, drop, replay, redirect; TLS gives secrecy and integrity, not delivery), ADV-14 (holder of the operator's
205-  approval device or key). Steps 3-4 defend against ADV-1..ADV-14.
206-- **Why:** each acts where the new parts sit (circuits, card exchange, approvals); the record shows hubs going wrong
207-  (stray hub 2026-10-04, G-060); both reviewers asked for ADV-14. Scored A +37, B +26, D −21, C −32.
208-- **Rejected:** B (drops ADV-13; "covered by TLS" misreads TLS, Codex r1), C (leaves CAND-17 and the approval device
209-  undefended), D (step 3 would not know whom the floor stops).
210-- **Settles directly:** no RR or CR; scopes RR-4, RR-14, RR-16, RR-18 and CR-14.
211-
212-### 2026-10-07 — OQ-2 the shared operating-system user (operator ruling)
213-- **Fact added at the brief:** all 19 Claude agents on .107 run as root (ps, 2026-10-07), so today the shared user is
214-  root and same-user equals host root in practice.
215-- **Chose:** C now, B as the committed target (operator's words: "we accept it for now, but we definitely want to move
216-  to a more isolated per-agent account, which can be created by the orchestrator … always via Tier 0"). (1) RR-2 is
217-  accepted for the present, with the root fact. (2) Target: one isolated OS account per agent, created by the
218-  orchestrator through a narrow privileged helper (least privilege: one helper with fixed settings, e.g. a single
219-  sudoers entry; never general root for the orchestrator); every creation is a Tier 0 action (human-approved, logged).
220-  (3) Step 3 phases and prices the move, including group-based access to shared checkouts; step 4 designs the helper.
221-  (4) Host root stays residual for the lifetime (RR-2).
222-- **Why:** one account per workload is the standard pattern (service users, systemd DynamicUser, k8s service accounts);
223-  it makes per-agent keys a real boundary and ends agents running as root. Scored C +29, A +10, B (as an immediate
224-  requirement) +5, D −12.
225-- **Rejected:** A (closes the door on separation), B now (unmeasured change blocking the floor), D (step 3 needs it).
226-- **Settles:** RR-2 accepted for now (with the target); RR-7 bounded by it.
227-
228-### 2026-10-07 — OQ-3 the circuit credential lifetime (operator ruling)
229-- **Evidence added at the brief:** the .107 hub journal (from 2026-10-04 only) shows 2 restarts in 3 days, down
230-  4m35s and ~1s; consistent with H-1 but thin.
231-- **Operator question answered first:** how is a sender authenticated if a credential can be stolen? Answer recorded:
232-  identity is the agent key pair (public key on the home hub's card, PR-1a); set-up is signed (7.2.a); the credential
233-  is sender-constrained and channel-bound (fresh proof of possession, 7.2.c); every message is signed (PR-1, CR-2).
234-  A stolen credential alone is useless; the remaining exposures are key theft (RR-2, OQ-2 target), a lying hub at
235-  first contact (RR-14, ADV-11, OQ-8), and no operator key yet (GP-11, OQ-6).
236-- **Chose:** D — PN-1 1 hour, PN-2 renewal from 30 minutes and 24 hours absolute, PN-13 1 hour, and the R-7.e (1)
237-  cross-host bound of 1 hour, as STARTING values; re-checked against 30 days of hub outage data (follow-up T-3383).
238-- **Why:** survives the measured outages with margin; a thief also needs the agent key; numbers stay open to evidence.
239-  Scored D +42, B +26, C +12, A −21.
240-- **Rejected:** A (a measured 4.5-minute restart takes a third of it), C (a working afternoon for a key thief),
241-  B without a dated re-check (rests on 3 days of one hub).
242-- **Settles:** PN-1, PN-2, PN-13, the R-7.e (1) bound; RR-5 accepted at a one-hour window.
243-
244-### 2026-10-08 — OQ-4 same-host new conversation without the hub (J4 question; operator ruling)
245-- **Chose:** D — A now: a new conversation always goes through the hub, on one host too (R-7.e (2) unchanged); only
246-  established conversations run on a circuit. Revisit B (local start while the hub is down) once per-agent OS
247-  accounts exist (OQ-2 target) and T-3383 has measured hub-down time.
248-- **Why:** a local start would be a second authority (R-45, R-49); with every agent running as root, "same user"
249-  cannot tell agents apart (7.6.b); the OQ-2 target removes that obstacle, so B gets a second look then. Scored
250-  D +42, A +33, B +9, C −17.
251-- **Rejected:** B now (trusts anyone on the host), C (bypasses the hub's checks permanently), A with no revisit.
252-- **Settles:** RR-11 accepted for now; TB-6 analysis (14.7, written for A) stands.
253-
254-### 2026-10-08 — OQ-5 failures the store must survive (GP-13; operator ruling)
255-- **Chose:** A — survive F-1 (sidecar killed mid-write) and F-2 (host crash: flush before STORED); detect and show
256-  F-3 (disk full: refuse STORED visibly), F-4 (corrupt record: checksum, quarantine, resend by digest), F-5 (store lost
257-  or old copy restored: reconcile with the hub record, flag lost-after-STORED); refuse or detect F-6 (second writer,
258-  lock) and F-7 (attacker writes; prevention is RR-2).
259-- **Why:** STORED releases the sender, so it must hold through a power cut; every other failure is loud, never
260-  silent. Scored A +42, C +32, B −18.
261-- **Rejected:** B (no flush; breaks the STORED promise in a crash for unmeasured speed), C (duplicate writes for a
